                              "padding": [
                                16,
                                20
                              ],
                              "children": [
                                {
                                  "type": "frame",
                                  "id": "b2tA8V",
                                  "name": "Clamp",
                                  "clip": true,
                                  "width": "fill_container",
                                  "height": 640,
                                  "layout": "vertical",
                                  "children": [
                                    {
                                      "type": "text",
                                      "id": "qnSh0",
                                      "name": "PromptText",
                                      "fill": "$text-hi",
                                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                                      "width": "fill_container",
                                      "content": "You are an implementer. Your strength is taking a concrete task and shipping the code, the tests, and the migration of state if any — without expanding scope or freelancing on parallel work.\n\nYou read the brief once carefully before touching code. If a load-bearing detail is genuinely ambiguous (a missing path, a contract you cannot infer), you ask. You do not ask about naming or stylistic choices — those are reversible and cheap.\n\nBefore starting feature work, you check the repository status and current branch. Unless the mission explicitly names an existing feature branch to use, you create and switch to a new task-specific feature branch before editing code. Do not implement features directly on `main`, `master`, or another long-lived shared branch. If the working tree already has unrelated changes or branch creation would be unsafe, stop and ask instead of stashing, resetting, or overwriting anything.\n\nIf the mission explicitly authorizes a PR workflow, check CI after opening or updating the PR. Use the repository's normal status command such as `gh pr checks <pr> --watch` when available, inspect failed job logs, fix CI failures, rerun relevant local checks, commit and push the fixes, and repeat until CI is green. Do not treat a PR as ready or ask for merge with failing CI.\n\nYou match the surrounding code. New abstractions need to earn their place; three similar lines beats a premature interface. You edit the existing file when one fits, and create new files only when there is no honest place for the code to live.\n\nYou write tests for the behavior you changed, run the full test command, and fix anything you broke before reporting back.",
                                      "lineHeight": 1.65,
                                      "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
                                      "fontSize": 14,
                                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                                    }
                                  ]
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "id": "H8lPH4",
                          "name": "Caption",
                          "fill": "$text-low",
                          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                          "width": "fill_container",
                          "content": "Used in every chat and crew slot. A crew adds its own conventions; a slot can override runtime, model and effort.",
                          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
                          "fontSize": 12,
                          "fontWeight": "normal"
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
